      "text": "Partition Tolerance ",
      "fontSize": 200.68872284337857,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Partition Tolerance ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "COcY1kE0lZzq6UFdCe7m9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1404.005870726029,
      "y": 10474.707897939756,
      "width": 5500.326192613768,
      "height": 1728.6341554514001,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#3bc9db",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aU",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1652380240,
      "version": 798,
      "versionNonce": 147114064,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1794731730181,
      "created": 1794731489586,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The CAP Theorem Trade-off: A distributed data store can provide at most two\nof three guarantees: Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance. Because\npartition tolerance cannot be sacrificed in a distributed network, the real design\nchoice during a partition is between:• CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance):\nThe system rejects or delays operations on disconnected nodes to prevent\nserving stale or conflicting data, sacrificing availability.\n• AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance): All functioning nodes keep accepting\nreads and writes, sacrificing strict consistency until the network heals and data\neventually syncs.",
      "fontSize": 153.6563693734578,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The CAP Theorem Trade-off: A distributed data store can provide at most two of three guarantees: Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance. Because partition tolerance cannot be sacrificed in a distributed network, the real design choice during a partition is between:• CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance): The system rejects or delays operations on disconnected nodes to prevent serving stale or conflicting data, sacrificing availability.\n• AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance): All functioning nodes keep accepting reads and writes, sacrificing strict consistency until the network heals and data eventually syncs.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
